 Well, if we can do that, why can't we get to an asteroid and put a probe on it and potentially mine it?

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:19.440
 And so I think the time is right.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:23.840
 And I've started to hear other people make this point on X.

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:25.740
 And people are like, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:27.440
 But you heard it first.

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:28.040
 Yeah.

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.840
 The OGs, yeah.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.140
 A little over a year ago, you started PsychoPunk.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:35.440
 That was, in retrospect, exceptional timing.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:39.840
 What was the decision factor in starting it at that time specifically?

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:44.240
 So we wanted to give people access to Zcash.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:47.140
 So Gemini listed Zcash in 2020.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:48.840
 And then in 2018.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:53.040
 And so I think we were probably the first regulated exchange ever to list it.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:54.740
 And we were very proud of that.

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:57.640
 And we've been supporting Zcash ever since.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:03.240
 But a lot of people, they need to access assets through an equity wrapper, let's say.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:10.740
 So we wanted to increase the ability for people to gain exposure to Zcash.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:16.940
 So we started by essentially buying Zcash and then expanding.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:19.640
 And that's how we became PsychoPunk, the company.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:26.640
 The goal is to support PsychoPunk ethos and invest in privacy technologies.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:27.140
 Right.

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:28.940
 So it's definitely not a debt.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:33.340
 It started by buying Zcash, but now we're mining.

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:46.740
 And ultimately, we want to support, invest in, foster privacy-promoting technology, technologies that promote the First Amendment, the Second Amendment,

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.840
 the Fourth Amendment, American values.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:54.840
 So it starts with Zcash, then it's evolved to Zcash, Zcash mining.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:19:00.340
 But really anything privacy, any privacy technology is on the table.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:04.640
 So how do you think of the politics around Zcash?

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:09.240
 I mean, obviously, there's been like a war on crypto in the past.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:12.340
 Is there a risk of having like a war on privacy?

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:15.240
 I mean, I'm thinking, for example, of vlog cameras.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:16.540
 Yeah. What are your thoughts on that?

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.740
 I think there probably will be a war on privacy.

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:22.140
 The question is not if, it's when.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:26.640
 But I think that Zcash is unstoppable private money.

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.140
 And we made the same arguments about Bitcoin.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.140
 It's very hard to stop Bitcoin.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:37.240
 It's easier than Zcash to stop Bitcoin, but it is very hard to stop decentralized money.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.340
 You pretty much have to stop the internet.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:44.040
 So I think we will win the war, but I think there unfortunately probably will be one.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:44.640
 I agree.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.340
 We were actually talking about this earlier.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:49.940
 Today, the war on crypto happened.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:52.940
 It was, I don't know, maybe it was four years.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:57.240
 But there already is a war, a quiet war, let's say, on privacy.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:03.440
 So going to the vlog cameras, a lot of people don't actually understand what they're doing.

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:12.940
 They think they've confused them with the cameras that take a picture of your license plate after you've broken the speed limit.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:14.840
 And they've conflated the two.

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:16.140
 They think they're the same.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:25.140
 It's completely different because one is focusing on your license plate and after you've allegedly broken the speed limit.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:31.740
 The other is surveilling everything and there's no trigger.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:35.740
 There's no allegedly breaking the law.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:39.740
 It's just always on and always surveilling.

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:45.940
 I've had a number of conversations where people thought they were the same thing.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:50.940
 It started with the license plate and speeding.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:57.340
 You can argue that, fine, but let's just table that argument whether that should even happen or not.

00:20:57.340 --> 00:21:06.140
 And of course, it only moved and progressed into we'll surveil everything, not just the license plate, but the car.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:10.740
 And law-abiding citizens who are just going to work and going about their daily lives.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:14.440
 And we're also going to put it in a centralized database.

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:15.740
 Mm-hmm.

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:16.940
 It's all stitched together.

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:25.640
 It's not like, hey, here's the traffic light camera of one municipality or city or town.

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:35.740
 It's sort of strung all together in a centralized database with AI tooling, right, where you can just put it together even better.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:39.740
 And ultimately, we know where that ends.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:45.540
 When you centralize data, it's a good target and people are going to want to hack it.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:54.040
 So you have all this information on people that's just sitting and being collected by the government, and that's probably not going to end well.

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:58.440
 I guess how we got to that was the war on privacy is always happening.

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:06.240
 Governments only want to get more powerful, larger trample on your rights.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:10.140
 That's just the only direction it goes.

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:15.340
 But will the war on privacy be explicit?

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:16.840
 Like the war on crypto?

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:17.840
 Mm-hmm.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:18.840
 Yes.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:28.840
 I mean, if history is any indication, I think that just like the internet, the government fought encryption and they treated it as a munition.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:32.340
 And they're completely wrong.

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:43.340
 And then eventually they understood that encryption is important and the internet can only thrive and work if there actually is encryption on it.

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:45.140
 So we analogize.

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:50.640
 We analogize Zcash to -- or let's say Bitcoin is HTTP.

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:55.640
 It's Web 1 where you put your credit card in to buy a plane ticket.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:57.140
 It's in the clear.

00:22:57.140 --> 00:23:02.140
 Everyone can read your information, your PII.

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:09.640
 And then Zcash is like HTTPS, secure, SSL, TLS, encrypted.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:13.140
 And the government fought this for a while.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:14.940
 And then eventually it completed.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:24.940
 It completely changed its tune and 180ed on this and said, oh, all government websites must actually be HTTPS.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:25.940
 Right.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:31.940
 So there's probably good reason to believe that they will start fighting it.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:34.940
 They'll realize it's inevitable.

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:37.940
 They can't shut it down without shutting down the internet.

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:38.940
 Can't really do that.

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:44.740
 And then they'll accept the fact that they have to work and live with it.

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:47.740
 So ultimately I think we win, but I think there will be a fight.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:54.740
 Yeah, I think ultimately the government will realize that Zcash is safer for citizens.

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:56.740
 And they will come around to that.

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.740
 But it will probably take time.

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:07.740
 But if we look at the number of attacks in crypto, pipe wrench attacks, I think there's been 40 in France alone this year.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:12.740
 And so having your money open on the blockchain, is it dangerous?

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.540
 It's not good for your citizens.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:21.540
 The same is a centralized large surveillance database on your citizens' movements and whereabouts.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.540
 That's also not a good thing.

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:32.540
 And I think the government hopefully will come around to the idea that it's actually not a good thing to centralize all this information or be so public and so open about it.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:34.540
 But it will take time, to Tyler's point.

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:37.540
 Like encryption, that arc took a while.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:43.540
 And now governments will advise citizens to use things like encryption to protect themselves.

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:44.340
 Yeah.

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:49.340
 They still probably hate it, but have accepted that it's the better alternative.

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:52.340
 And I guess there's also like little they can do against this.

00:24:52.340 --> 00:25:03.340
 I mean, I think I remember people printing PGP keys on paper because it's basically free speech and circumventing these restrictions that way.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:08.340
 And similarly, it's very hard to stop Zcash and technologies like Bitcoin, as you said, Cameron.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:14.140
 I guess as a final question, one of the general remarks people have with regards to privacy is,

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:16.140
 "Oh, I have nothing to hide."

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.140
 What's your rebuttal to that?

00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:25.140
 I think of, so why did Satoshi not put his name to the white paper in Bitcoin?

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:31.140
 And I think he's a great example where ideas like Bitcoin are dangerous.

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:42.140
 And he clearly realized that putting his own identity to Bitcoin was dangerous for him personally, from a safety perspective.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:43.940
 And so...

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:55.940
 Or you can go back hundreds of years to Copernicus and his dangerous idea that the earth actually revolves around the sun and not vice versa.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:26:01.940
 And that was a very dangerous idea when he first came up with it.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:13.740
 So there's many reasons why privacy fosters intellectual curiosity, discoveries, innovation, ideas that are cutting edge.

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:22.740
 That challenge the status quo, challenge the government, challenge our worldview, how we think about things.

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.740
 And there's many, many examples of that.

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:29.740
 You know, we could just continue to look back in history along the way.

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:32.740
 But I think Satoshi is the most obvious example.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:41.740
 And also Satoshi, I think the Bitcoin that Satoshi mined, he couldn't even move it at this point because it's sitting there.

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:43.540
 And it's just too dangerous.

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:44.540
 Too dangerous to move.

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:48.540
 So I think that those are the reasons I would say.

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:51.540
 Yeah, I mean, you might say like, oh, I don't need privacy.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:27:01.540
 But you want people like Satoshi to have privacy because they might be inventing Bitcoin that you benefit from.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:09.540
 And so, yes, like Bitcoin is very threatening to governments, central banks, power structures.

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:13.340
 And you may not have it without privacy.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:18.340
 So a lot of Einstein's ideas challenged the establishment.

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:22.340
 And he actually wasn't in a university at the time.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:27.340
 He was working as a patent officer in Bern, Switzerland.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:36.340
 So he didn't have to worry about ruffling feathers of the professors above him that might be upset and blackball him for tenure.

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:38.340
 He wasn't in that political structure.

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:43.140
 And that gave him freedom of thought, right, to think outside of the box.

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:44.140
 Let's say.

00:27:44.140 --> 00:27:51.140
 But also freedom from political retribution on his career path.

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:58.140
 And so ultimately, he didn't need privacy to publish his papers.

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:00.140
 But there still might have been resistance.

00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:06.140
 And maybe he would have needed it if he was a professor in a university system.

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:12.940
 And so there's many reasons why privacy is important, even if you don't think you need it for yourself.

00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:25.940
 Just like I really like what Snowden says about he has a riff on freedom of speech and saying that it's something about, like, I don't care about it because I have nothing good to say.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:26.940
 I'm getting it wrong.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:34.940
 But freedom of speech is important, even if you don't want to exercise it or you don't.

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:39.940
 Maybe you're not going on X and you just, you know, not using it.

00:28:39.940 --> 00:28:42.740
 You still want that free marketplace of ideas.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:48.740
 You still want it to to occur and to happen because it's better for society.

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:49.740
 It's better for outcomes.

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:50.740
 It's better for innovation.

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:52.740
 It's better for progress.

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:57.740
 It's more likely to build an iPhone that you may purchase and may use.

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:12.540
 And so there's all these knock on effects that make privacy, freedom of speech, freedom, liberty super valuable to you, even if you don't think you need to exercise them directly yourself.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.540
 And I think it avoids people from, like, self censoring.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:27.540
 If you think of, like, the current state of academia, like how many how many professors and intellectuals have to censor their actual beliefs and what they're doing because they'll get canceled.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:28.540
 Exactly.

00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:33.540
 So it's very important for freedom of thought and that type of inquiry.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:37.540
 And it should, at a minimum, be sort of default privacy.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:38.540
 And you can opt out.

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:42.340
 But default public is not where we want to be.

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:47.340
 It also forces people to engage on the idea.

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:48.340
 Right.

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:51.340
 So let's say Satoshi wasn't worried about his safety.

00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:58.340
 But if you there's only one thing to look at, which is the source code, you can't attack the person.

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:02.340
 You can't, like, confuse the two or conflate the two.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:08.340
 You have to focus on the idea and the contents and separate that.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:12.140
 And that's super powerful because it's not about judging.

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:15.140
 The person behind the idea, it's just you have to focus.

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:22.140
 You have no choice but to speak to the idea and the facts and its merit.

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:27.140
 And so privacy focuses the conversation to where it should be.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:28.140
 Right.

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:29.140
 That makes sense.

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:30.140
 Yeah.

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:32.140
 And that's why, you know, people use soothes on X.

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:41.940
 Maybe not because they want to not be public, but they just want you to have to address, like, what they're saying in the tweets.

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:43.940
 And just stick it there.

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:44.940
 Right.

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:45.940
 Yeah.

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:46.940
 Thank you, guys.

00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:47.940
 It's been a pleasure.

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:48.940
 Thank you.

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:49.940
 Thanks for having us.

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:50.940
 Great questions.

